Subject: Case-Convert ASCII Strings Without The Unicode Machinery

What this does
  js_string_toLowerCase scans a narrow string's bytes first. When every byte is
  ASCII it either returns the original string, if no byte would change, or
  allocates once and maps the bytes in place.

Why we need it
  The Unicode path walks the string with string_getc, calls lre_case_conv into a
  result buffer for every code point and appends through a StringBuffer, even
  when the answer is the string it was given. ASCII is the common case in
  case-insensitive comparison, formatters and token normalisation.

What it adds
  Any byte >= 0x80 falls through to the existing Unicode path, so the final-sigma
  rule, the dotted-I expansion and every multi-character mapping are unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -53353,6 +53353,44 @@
     p = JS_VALUE_GET_STRING(val);
     if (p->len == 0)
